.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
This "help" flag cause the program to print out a description
of its operation and then exit immediately.
.TP
.B \-\-input=\fIinputGridFile\fP
.br
Sets the filename of the input topography grid to be tiled. This must be
a GMT/netCDF grid readable by the \fBMBIO\fP grid I/O routines. This option
(or the equivalent short option \fB\-I\fP) must be specified.
.TP
.B \-\-output=\fIoutputRoot\fP
.br
Sets the root name used both for the output directory that is created to
hold the tile grids, octree files, and "tiles.csv" catalog, and for the
basenames of the individual tile files written into that directory. This
option (or the equivalent short option \fB\-O\fP) must be specified.
.TP
.B \-\-tile\-dimension=\fItileDimension\fP, \fB\-D\fP\fItileDimension\fP
.br
Sets the number of grid nodes along each side of a (square) output tile.
If neither this option nor \fB\-\-tile\-spacing\fP is given, \fItileDimension\fP
defaults to 2001. If \fB\-\-tile\-spacing\fP is also given, \fB\-\-tile\-spacing\fP
takes precedence and \fItileDimension\fP is instead derived from it.
.TP
.B \-\-tile\-spacing=\fItileSpacing\fP, \fB\-S\fP\fItileSpacing\fP
.br
Sets the desired tile half\-width in the input grid's horizontal coordinate
units (typically meters). When given, the tile dimension (number of nodes
per side) is calculated from \fItileSpacing\fP and the input grid's node
spacing, overriding any value set with \fB\-\-tile\-dimension\fP.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
By default (\fB\-\-verbose\fP not given) \fBmbgrdtilemaker\fP prints only a modest
summary: a line identifying the input grid and, at the end of the run, a
line giving the number of tiles generated and the output location. Giving
\fB\-\-verbose\fP (or \fB\-V\fP) at least once raises the level of detail reported,
causing \fBmbgrdtilemaker\fP to also print the full input grid parameters,
the full output tileset parameters, and a line per tile and per
\fBmbgrd2octree\fP invocation as the tiles are generated. Repeating
\fB\-\-verbose\fP a second time additionally redirects all of this output to
stderr instead of stdout and adds a further level of debug detail.

